**Budget (BIOBUZZ R503): 8 motors + 8 servos total across ALL configurations.**
Current use: **<n>/8 motors, <n>/8 servos.** If a change would add an actuator,
say so explicitly before writing code — it may be illegal, not just expensive.

## Units — one convention, no exceptions
- Distance: **inches**. Angles: **degrees** at every API boundary; radians only
  inside a single math method, converted on the way in and out.
- Motor position: **encoder ticks**; convert at the subsystem edge with a named
  constant from `Constants`, never inline.
- Time: **seconds** (`ElapsedTime.seconds()`), never milliseconds.
- Field frame: <origin and +X / +Y / heading convention — or "TBD until kickoff">

Any variable holding a converted value carries its unit in its name:
`liftHeightIn`, `armAngleDeg`, `targetTicks`, `elapsedSec`. **Reject any change
where a bare number crosses a unit boundary.**

## Where things go
| Path | Contents |
|---|---|
| `config/Constants.java` | EVERY tunable number. No numeric literal anywhere else except 0 and 1. |
| `config/HardwareNames.java` | EVERY `hardwareMap` string. |
| `subsystems/` | One class per mechanism. Owns its hardware. Exposes intent, not implementation. |
| `opmodes/auto/`, `opmodes/teleop/` | Name autos for what they score, not for a code word. |
| `tuning/` | One OpMode per mechanism, kept all season. Instrumentation, not throwaway code. |
| `util/MathFunctions.java` | Pure static math, **no SDK imports**, so it is unit-testable. |
| `TeamCode/src/test/java/` | JUnit 5. Any pure function you write gets a test. |
| `docs/HARDWARE.md`, `docs/TUNING-LOG.md` | Human-facing wiring notes and the dated tuning journal. |

## Subsystem contract — every subsystem obeys all six
1. Constructor takes `HardwareMap`; it does all `hardwareMap.get()` calls and
   nothing else expensive.
2. Exactly one `periodic()`, called once per loop from the OpMode.
3. Public methods express **intent** (`setState(State.SCORE)`), never raw power.
4. Exposes `atTarget()` so state machines and autos can sequence on it.
5. Never reads the gamepad. Never calls `telemetry.update()`. Never calls
   `sleep()` or `Thread.sleep()`.
6. Never blocks. If a behaviour takes time it is a state machine, not a wait.

## Safety rules you must follow when writing robot code
These are not style preferences. Violating one of these breaks hardware or hurts
a person.
- Every closed-loop output is clamped: `Range.clip(out, -MAX, MAX)`, MAX from `Constants`.
- Every position setpoint is clamped to a soft limit from `Constants` **before**
  it reaches hardware.
- Servos: `setPosition()` arguments must lie inside the SAFE RANGE in the
  hardware table. **Never widen a servo range. Never call `scaleRange()` to make
  a target "fit".** If a target is outside the range, STOP and report it.
- Every `while` loop in a `LinearOpMode` has `opModeIsActive()` or
  `!isStopRequested()` in its condition.
- New mechanism motors get `motor.setCurrentAlert(<A>, CurrentUnit.AMPS)` and the
  subsystem checks `isMotorOverCurrent()` and cuts power. Control Hub motor ports
  are 10 A continuous / 20 A peak.
- Voltage-compensate anything tuned: scale output by `12.0 / batteryVoltage`.
- Bulk reads on for both hubs: `setBulkCachingMode(BulkCachingMode.AUTO)`.
- Loop time is always on driver-station telemetry. If a change could slow the
  loop, say so in your summary.

## Numbers you may NOT invent
PID/PIDF gains · feedforward constants · servo positions · encoder-to-inch
ratios · arm angles · timing constants · camera offsets · field coordinates ·
gear ratios · spool diameters.

If a number is needed and is not already in `Constants.java`, write a
clearly-marked placeholder — `= 0.0; // TODO MEASURE: <what and how>` — and tell
the human **what to measure and with what procedure.** Never copy a gain from
another team's repo, from a tutorial, or from your own estimate. A plausible
wrong gain is more dangerous than an obvious zero.

## Test-before-deploy protocol — run every step, in order
No code reaches the robot until all five pass. State which steps you actually
ran; **compiling is not testing.**
1. `./gradlew :TeamCode:compileDebugJavaWithJavac` — must be clean, zero warnings you introduced.
2. `./gradlew :TeamCode:test` — must be green. New pure logic gets a new test in the same change.
3. **Self-review against the safety list above**, naming each rule you checked.
4. **State the physical failure mode in one sentence**: "if this is wrong, the
   <mechanism> will <what> and could <damage what>." If you cannot name one, you
   do not understand the change well enough to ship it.
5. **Bench test before field test.** Robot on blocks, wheels off the ground,
   mechanism at its safest position, a human on the STOP button. Only then the field.

After a robot test, the human reports back and we log the result in
`docs/TUNING-LOG.md` with the date, the value changed, and the measured effect.
Never write "tested and working" yourself — you cannot see the robot.

## Ask before changing — the boundaries
**Stop and ask a human** before you:
- change any number in `Constants.java` that was measured or tuned;
- change a hardware name, port, direction, or anything in the hardware table;
- add, remove or repurpose a motor or servo (R503 budget, see above);
- add a library, bump a version, or touch any Gradle file;
- delete or rewrite an OpMode that has been run at an event;
- refactor code you were not asked to touch, or "clean up" something adjacent;
- change anything in `tuning/` (those OpModes are our measuring instruments);
- do anything that would require a laptop connected during a match.

**Proceed without asking** for: new code in a new file, adding a unit test,
adding telemetry behind `COMPETITION_MODE`, fixing a compile error in code you
just wrote, adding a comment, formatting your own new code.

When a request is ambiguous — **especially about hardware, geometry, or a
number** — ask. One question costs 30 seconds. A wrong guess costs a mechanism.

## Competition mode
`Constants.COMPETITION_MODE` gates every dashboard / telemetry-streaming code path.
**Rule R704.D names FTC Dashboard and FTControl Panels as prohibited on the Robot
Controller Wi-Fi network, and R704.C requires all programming laptops to be
disconnected during MATCH play.** Debugging at events is by on-robot file logging,
pulled over USB afterwards. **Never add a code path that requires a laptop
connection in order to work.**

## Git
- `main` is always what is on the robot right now.
- One short-lived branch per feature: `feat/lift-pid`, `fix/claw-range`.
- Commit message: `<subsystem>: what changed` — e.g. `lift: raise kG to 0.0009 after new spool`.
- Tuning-value changes get their own commits, separate from logic changes, so we
  can bisect a regression to a number.
- You may `git add` and `git commit`. You may NOT `git push`, `git reset --hard`,
  `git clean`, or rebase.

## How to work with us
- We are students learning to program. **Explain what you did and why, in plain
  language, every time.** If we cannot explain your code to a judge, we cannot use it.
- We must be able to defend every design decision in a judge interview
  (BIOBUZZ A201; see `research/AI-IN-FTC-POLICY.md`). When you make a
  non-obvious choice, give us the one-sentence reason we would say out loud.
- Prefer boring, inspectable code over clever code.
- When you finish, say in one sentence what could physically go wrong on the
  robot if you are wrong.

## Keep this file fresh
If you are corrected twice on the same thing, propose an edit to this file.
If a mechanism is rebuilt, the hardware table and safe ranges above are **stale
until a human updates them** — say so rather than trusting them.

